#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228879 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c51535d3-6b6a-5df3-a41f-9bad8e2edfbe" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit"}] :question/id #uuid "353e21f2-2d1d-55e3-ac86-19932d117997" :question/math-academy-id "e-21759" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/7e54c9613c18a0ea26df6a4088598b566e532fba555dc08db0bcfd539b4c64d5.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $80\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.0025\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 80\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 80I = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.0025} = 400q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\sin(3t) = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 400q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\sin(3t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228880 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "28fd19b3-762a-572e-9b52-27e17093ad93" :question/math-academy-id "e-21762" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/424cb52325c95f03708ed9ae5fe1d41e7b0d1c8bb8878e1dd8dc002aef214481.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\textrm{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 80\\,\\Omega.$So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 80I = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.00125} = 800q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n24 = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 800q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q = \\dfrac3{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + 10\\alpha = \\dfrac3{10}\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = \\dfrac3{100}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $q_p(t) = \\dfrac3{100}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-10t} + \\dfrac3{100}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\textrm{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0) = \\dfrac{1}{50},$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{50} \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{3}{100} & =\\frac{1}{50} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{100}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{100}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{3}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{100}(3-{e}^{-10t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c = -\\dfrac1{100}e^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The constant $q_p = \\dfrac3{100}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to\\infty,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228881 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "a3e7a060-f36d-5fd6-a6f6-5b321261fb0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21763" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/962694e1706c47a4b060da6636e0b6e2f1653cb94137dca8dc59781cf74bd252.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $90\\cos(6t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\dfrac1{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac1{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n90\\cos(6t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = 3\\cos(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(6t) + \\beta\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(5\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{15}{61}, \\beta = \\dfrac{18}{61}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} + \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{15}{61}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{18}{61}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.38,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f5d828-8aac-5c11-a700-6a224351dc02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.009" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332244} {:answer/id #uuid "f402933f-dbff-572b-900e-87080334bc6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332245} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d5a913-a242-5a8b-8740-83f1589c540e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.071" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332246} {:answer/id #uuid "07c2bd30-9f8f-57ec-852d-195930a725d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332247} {:answer/id #uuid "38891204-b51f-5a2f-a615-fe9ff0e9931d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07c2bd30-9f8f-57ec-852d-195930a725d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332247} :answer-field/id #uuid "644135f7-c6a0-5f4f-b370-e523ea57caea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "70f691e9-1983-578a-ad38-54612631ed7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336590" :question/problem "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\nFind, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.05$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{80}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}q=\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{25}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{5}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{40}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{40}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3}+\\frac{3}{40}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{80}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{80},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{80} \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)/3}+\\frac{3}{40} & =\\frac{1}{80} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{3}{40}-\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-25t/3}\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.05$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.05)=\\frac{3}{40}-\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-25(0.05)/3}≈0.034,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7054326-84f5-5bd0-9574-d49bdee73626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332249} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a01fda-5559-5014-b0e4-024be3d2d17c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8a01fda-5559-5014-b0e4-024be3d2d17c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332250} :answer-field/id #uuid "e518865b-fc46-5d44-87dc-da0fa2424fde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9547864c-5c79-5eab-ae35-a6351dc3262f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336594" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+6q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+6{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{1}{12}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{1}{12}$ coulombs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c51535d3-6b6a-5df3-a41f-9bad8e2edfbe" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbfcf617-857a-5a1e-a2fe-7f9dfae2efe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332253} {:answer/id #uuid "1bbad904-c865-51df-b5ee-6cc8e7252067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{10}{3}q=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332254} {:answer/id #uuid "baffbf3f-578f-53e3-80ac-91ba1604d0eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332255} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b170b0-8a94-5f75-afec-e12d167de96b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{10}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332256} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2ede87-a2ab-569d-9a92-cdc4401e8a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6b170b0-8a94-5f75-afec-e12d167de96b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{10}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332256} :answer-field/id #uuid "1034b298-4230-5ca9-a49a-4352c9d15f98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83e77621-8cb6-50e5-8ae8-f075ea7afe42" :question/math-academy-id "q-337039" :question/problem "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.01\\,\\text{F}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{10}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c51535d3-6b6a-5df3-a41f-9bad8e2edfbe" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "934f5940-e60b-561b-8964-5af58f2a31cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{3}{50}q=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332260} {:answer/id #uuid "946cce7b-4cb1-5612-ab15-6c9908885d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q=\\frac{3}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332261} {:answer/id #uuid "39b72d30-e7cf-5ab0-ba97-f46b58b03b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+q=\\frac{3}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332262} {:answer/id #uuid "1689a192-1421-5dc9-8fad-6dbd8ba3a3b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+q=30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332263} {:answer/id #uuid "138d6c45-c5b2-5eca-b48d-8d55aa453a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{250}q=30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39b72d30-e7cf-5ab0-ba97-f46b58b03b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+q=\\frac{3}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332262} :answer-field/id #uuid "101f41db-32bc-565e-96b7-68d7c7b5df47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be319b2e-b723-5164-af9f-5256928dd09d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337053" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/2cccb2a1edfeed0a562303053395c6599e14cbf08f9dc5e7f07a4d657755b1ae.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $250\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.004\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=250\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=250I=250\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15=250\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+q=\\frac{3}{50}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c51535d3-6b6a-5df3-a41f-9bad8e2edfbe" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a02e2688-6f39-5513-840b-eebe955e7576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+50q=\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332267} {:answer/id #uuid "3e261c7b-65a7-5e55-91c8-c88fcdb10df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332268} {:answer/id #uuid "1fcc9b4e-8142-5d8b-a4dd-02e0fd26f4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332269} {:answer/id #uuid "d685ff90-df84-5f7d-b7ef-62ea3ef48b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332270} {:answer/id #uuid "500fe9f4-0386-59e5-91ac-cb8806505de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "500fe9f4-0386-59e5-91ac-cb8806505de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332271} :answer-field/id #uuid "15c864bf-cfbe-52d0-8d7b-7dcbf9217425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5cf6de37-864a-51b9-9bef-fcb14b769367" :question/math-academy-id "q-337062" :question/problem "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $50\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.002\\,\\text{F}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c51535d3-6b6a-5df3-a41f-9bad8e2edfbe" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b31e68a-e510-578a-888c-86734cf60cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332274} {:answer/id #uuid "e40203fc-ec03-58d5-bc5b-78a7d2941a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+80q=\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332275} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc3c662-e47d-5d56-8b57-f4b7a1b3ba7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+800q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332276} {:answer/id #uuid "36a8d11b-8f36-58df-a06f-52d1a72fad33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+800q=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332277} {:answer/id #uuid "2c25ee68-50a3-5079-99e0-3caee8b280cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b31e68a-e510-578a-888c-86734cf60cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332274} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea64a2c8-97bc-50a3-befc-770653526291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fec00bec-d7d0-523c-a284-99e9cf18db59" :question/math-academy-id "q-337063" :question/problem "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $80\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.00125\\,\\text{F}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=80\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=80I=80\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.00125}=800q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=80\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+800q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c51535d3-6b6a-5df3-a41f-9bad8e2edfbe" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e5a9d63-4a18-527e-b170-9a802d9a8066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{9}{50}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332281} {:answer/id #uuid "c98350fd-f97b-555c-81bb-b8fb21864e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332282} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ebffca-1509-5de0-9a7a-5b0cf7414e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332283} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc14736-920b-54ba-ba06-657fd9bc7155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q=18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332284} {:answer/id #uuid "0388246f-0794-59e0-9dcd-72ff5d336ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1ebffca-1509-5de0-9a7a-5b0cf7414e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332283} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f11723b-f889-53f3-a33f-03d1c8b5c510" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "adf0cbd3-9460-53ad-9c10-ec9fcbd41337" :question/math-academy-id "q-337104" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/6f9417fdec3a123dbb28ae87a31c6dc49e38327178e1335030b51505f5ca2cbd.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $60\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.003\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=60\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=60I=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.003}=\\frac{1000}{3}q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "385c993c-62c4-57b5-b931-292b2c3a6ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "385c993c-62c4-57b5-b931-292b2c3a6ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332288} :answer-field/id #uuid "eacf1194-d909-547e-8612-617297057a96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa53f6c5-7b89-59b3-97f9-e5608374a440" :question/math-academy-id "q-337111" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." :question/worked-solution "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =\\frac{2}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{2}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{15}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{2}{15}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{2}{15}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{2}{15}$ coulombs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2db2a37b-cd78-56e2-a85a-e887302a8364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.048-0.008e^{-6.25t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db2a37b-cd78-56e2-a85a-e887302a8364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.048-0.008e^{-6.25t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332291} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfec99a5-8afa-5aa9-b275-239fb1e48d40" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fa211b0-0cd5-5330-8aaa-5e30de3b98c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-337114" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/b91e3582e017882aae67e8b6378074cf941be0c090d9f3f978076583b36c8837.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{25}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{25}{4}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{25}{4}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/4},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{25}{4}\\alpha =\\frac{3}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{6}{125}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{125}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/4}+\\frac{6}{125}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{25}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{25},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)/4}+\\frac{6}{125} & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{125}{e}^{-25t/4}+\\frac{6}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{125}(6-{e}^{-25t/4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{1}{125}{e}^{-25t/4}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{6}{125}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dee38b0f-57ad-5712-b583-1e883eae4906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.054" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332294} {:answer/id #uuid "51024ba2-8285-5bd6-8762-f6c781505714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.011" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332295} {:answer/id #uuid "e2dc2191-c5a8-5e6e-94d1-a23510eca715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332296} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0c23f5-9afe-57d1-8161-5cca7088342d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.027" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332297} {:answer/id #uuid "3b7629c0-5261-5877-98ee-5f1994f75c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.038" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a0c23f5-9afe-57d1-8161-5cca7088342d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.027" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332297} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e02ae75-6a59-5a1a-907c-fa13f9cc95a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5916b214-e7c4-5654-94af-c7367154a0c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-337124" :question/problem "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $18\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.006\\,\\text{F}.$ Find, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.05$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{90}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I=18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.006}=\\frac{500}{3}q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9=18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{500}{3}q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{250}{27}q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{250}{27}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-250t/27},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{250}{27}\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{27}{500}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{27}{500}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-250t/27}+\\frac{27}{500}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{90}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{90},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{90} \\\\ A{e}^{-250(0)/27}+\\frac{27}{500} & =\\frac{1}{90} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{193}{4500}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{27}{500}-\\frac{193}{4500}{e}^{-250t/27}\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.05$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.05)=\\frac{27}{500}-\\frac{193}{4500}{e}^{-250(0.05)/27}≈0.027,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15b70f96-d44a-523a-8022-a4033e2121bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332301} {:answer/id #uuid "c04855c0-9435-5b3e-9d02-008a8d2d02fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332302} {:answer/id #uuid "96426829-58c3-53ab-ac64-410c5699ecac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332303} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f097dc-2bc0-5868-904e-c94ca6e1d92c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332304} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d5d2a5-796a-54c2-b899-3ce0615a2297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c04855c0-9435-5b3e-9d02-008a8d2d02fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332302} :answer-field/id #uuid "6041abd6-0e19-5811-86cb-6153c87e4fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a590bef-41b8-5b91-a8cb-9d38b0f1d2fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-337138" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/0221452e6c995bd4d4e027452c9949d57afc181e99d549434fc48ce8e29ced4b.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{P}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{Q}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.004\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{29},\\beta =\\frac{2}{145}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=1,Q=2,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=1+2=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd2a6e65-88ac-596d-8bbc-8d7a6bb3967d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332308} {:answer/id #uuid "121fd18c-54a2-5772-a8ec-f9a7acdf2f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332309} {:answer/id #uuid "39058036-d388-5fb1-893c-47b6d95385fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332310} {:answer/id #uuid "67627340-31fc-5565-8050-f023b9a9baca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332311} {:answer/id #uuid "f04ff174-2bbd-50f4-ab77-2939081faf16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd2a6e65-88ac-596d-8bbc-8d7a6bb3967d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332308} :answer-field/id #uuid "683dab6c-8a88-58d6-9f50-3154a420a34e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a548052a-d55c-5532-a108-ec0e560d355b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337348" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/f1568a632a02f644f7ef8c39417bb06973f0862494aa0c3c624880a87c5620ee.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{100})}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $5t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $5t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+(4\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +4\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{10}{41},\\beta =\\frac{8}{41}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{10}{41})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{41})}^{2}}≈0.31,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee2f64e-d1a5-5c2e-99c8-cabb8b8dbf6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332315} {:answer/id #uuid "126b65ac-0efe-5d37-8915-bf9ff0697d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332316} {:answer/id #uuid "37d77814-5070-5b8e-b522-daae2f20d814" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332317} {:answer/id #uuid "94ac2926-d692-5048-a8d3-132a117f7a40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332318} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe92ab3-3d6d-53d2-91a0-eb63cb227c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d77814-5070-5b8e-b522-daae2f20d814" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332317} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d1a3c98-b844-5eed-8115-82fcb1be9381" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59badfb2-7502-5509-9bc2-489d76ce985a" :question/math-academy-id "q-337349" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/0bf264310dba0dc66332b3c8e0f2e1c780ca199e7d5ea3c1f21f5132a02e06dd.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{200})}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+8{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +8\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{9}{50}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{6}{25})}^{2}+{(\\frac{9}{50})}^{2}}=0.3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a54cf421-da4c-5f40-85f6-bc180c2348f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332322} {:answer/id #uuid "5a16cc0c-a04e-5308-bae1-8c21e6c1e4d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332323} {:answer/id #uuid "a167bd6b-33a3-55d1-ae0b-26bdf8638b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332324} {:answer/id #uuid "0b6e7307-bd40-5df5-afb0-4816245cf9fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332325} {:answer/id #uuid "a06051a9-1c40-596e-86f5-e03a76389d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a54cf421-da4c-5f40-85f6-bc180c2348f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332322} :answer-field/id #uuid "37608a12-5234-58df-bc5e-618705d40d7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fabf183a-0b74-50c7-8a81-ce24ea3a18bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-337350" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/d0841cfa21b90ba01207e42970b2f7e39c63c1df8c2b2dbd5154ae723ac5aa7a.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $18\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{72}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I=18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{72})}=72q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+72q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{8}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{6}{25})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{25})}^{2}}=0.4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9be4bcf1-d5f4-54b3-9072-c7d62891535c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332329} {:answer/id #uuid "15c5dcc9-576f-5830-89f7-2efb7a6277f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332330} {:answer/id #uuid "11845b54-8062-54f2-8e7a-9280f60f5adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332331} {:answer/id #uuid "a04c3b6e-63e0-58e1-b095-b4e4847c7882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332332} {:answer/id #uuid "96401b9c-0568-576c-b7ff-1e273c5b80ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9be4bcf1-d5f4-54b3-9072-c7d62891535c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332329} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb6c4e3e-96a1-5372-8503-144e3cb11a63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98fa0f37-09b6-54ca-a27f-e4495d3daa77" :question/math-academy-id "q-337352" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/b1933db5df10c1a3faf07f1d459a7736be682a3d01ab85e48214da21e7a9d1d5.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{100})}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(5\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +5\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{16}{41},\\beta =\\frac{20}{41}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{16}{41})}^{2}+{(\\frac{20}{41})}^{2}}≈0.62,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}]}
